mode: "100644"
text: "# Jobs To Be Done — the lens that beats personas\n\nMost audience research produces a demographic character sheet that never helps anyone write a\npost. This is the lens that does: people don't follow accounts because of their age — they\nfollow, read, and buy to make **progress** in their lives. Capture the progress, and you know\nwhat to make.\n\n## What a \"job\" is\n\nA job-to-be-done is the progress someone is trying to make in a given situation. They \"hire\"\ncontent (and products) to get that job done. Jobs come in three layers, and the best content\nhits more than one:\n\n- **Functional job** — the practical task. *\"Run payroll without messing up taxes.\"*\n- **Emotional job** — how they want to feel. *\"Stop feeling anxious and out of my depth.\"*\n- **Social job** — how they want to be seen. *\"Look competent to my team / not like an amateur.\"*\n\nA payroll tool's audience isn't hiring \"payroll software.\" They're hiring *\"get this scary thing\noff my plate so I can stop worrying and look like I have it together.\"* That sentence tells you\nwhat to post; \"35, urban, $80k income\" does not.\n\n## Why JTBD beats demographic personas\n\n| Demographic persona | Jobs-to-be-done |\n|---|---|\n| \"Marketing Mary, 35, likes coffee\" | \"Wants to prove content ROI to a skeptical boss\" |\n| Describes *who they are* | Describes *what they're trying to do* |\n| Rarely changes the content | Directly generates content angles |\n| Often invented | Grounded in real behavior |\n\nDemographics matter only when they actually change the content (region for a local shop,\nseniority for a B2B tool). Otherwise they're theater.\n\n## The three things to capture around each job\n\n- **Pains** — what blocks the job or makes it painful now. (The problem your content addresses.)\n- **Desires** — the outcome they actually want. (The promise your content makes.)\n- **Objections** — why they hesitate to act / believe / buy. (What content must answer\n  pre-emptively.)\n\nEvery pain is a content idea. Every objection is a post that removes friction. Every desire is a\ntransformation you can show.\n\n## How to find the jobs\n\n- **The \"what were they trying to do?\" question** — for any review, comment, or sale, ask what\n  progress they were after.\n- **The before → after** — what state were they in before the brand, and after? The gap *is* the\n  job.\n- **The switch** — what made them seek a solution / start following? The trigger reveals the job.\n- **Mine the evidence** — the voice-of-customer sources (`voice-of-customer.md`) are where jobs\n  show up in the audience's own words.\n\n## Turning jobs into content\n\nEach job and its pains/objections is a content angle:\n\n- Functional job → how-to / educational content.\n- Emotional job → reassurance, relatable, story content.\n- Social job → status, identity, \"look smart sharing this\" content.\n- Objection → myth-busting, proof, FAQ content.\n\nA content plan built from jobs is automatically relevant, because it's built from what the\naudience is actually trying to do.\n"
